# DURABLE REPRO — the STRUCT get-chain, `&`-FACE ONLY.
#
# This is the OTHER cell that survives the `&`-of-projection fix (the tuple
# get-chain, both faces, is `sound_tuple_getchain_writethrough.gg`). It is
# narrower than that one: here ONLY the `&` face is broken.
#
# WRONG TODAY (measured at HEAD, Rust gg, C and LLVM alike):
#   `&` face      `bumpi(&vb.get(0).unwrap().fd)`  -> prints 10   (want 11)
#   assign face   `wb.get(0).unwrap().fd = 42`     -> prints 42   ✅ ALREADY CORRECT
# `ggdef` prints 11 / 42 — correct on both, so it adjudicates AGAINST production
# on the `&` face. That makes this a Core #8 silent wrong-output.
#
# ⚠ THE ASSIGN ROW IS A LIVE CONTROL, NOT A GAP. It passes today and must KEEP
# passing: it is the row that proves the two faces take different paths. The
# assign face works because it falls back to `resolve_ptr_field_place`
# (stmts/assigns.rs), whose own comment documents the `.get().unwrap()` chain;
# the `&`-formation path has no such fallback, and
# `try_resolve_field_place`'s object arms (Identifier / SelfExpr / FieldAccess /
# Deref / Index) include no method-chain arm.
#
# INTENDED (what this fixture asserts): 11 then 42.
#
# ⚠ A fix that closes this must NOT be assumed to close the tuple get-chain —
# measured, it does not: `resolve_ptr_field_place` is struct-field-specific
# (it takes a `field_name: &str`), so the tuple case needs a tuple-index
# analogue. That measurement is why the get-chain fallback was descoped from
# the `&`-projection track rather than folded into it.
#
# The seeds are COMPUTED, not literals, so const-folding cannot elide the store.

struct B:
    int fd

int seed():
    return 10

void bumpi(int &x):
    x = x + 1

void main():
    Vector[B] vb = [B(seed())]
    bumpi(&vb.get(0).unwrap().fd)
    print(vb.get(0).unwrap().fd)

    Vector[B] wb = [B(seed())]
    wb.get(0).unwrap().fd = 42
    print(wb.get(0).unwrap().fd)
